<Case slug="allumove" chapters={chapters}>
<section id="context"><p class="k">01 / Context</p><h2>A score cannot explain what to do next.</h2><p>Allumove is a residential rental product with experiences for residents and brokers. Before a visit, a broker needs to understand whether a person’s circumstances fit a particular property—and what information is still missing.</p><p>The early prototype displayed a standalone score. It suggested a precise assessment without explaining its criteria. The product review replaced that score with an explicit, property-specific qualification.</p><div class="case-brief"><h3>My contribution</h3><p>Product decisions and interface development within a collaborative, AI-assisted project. This case focuses on the documented qualification redesign and its React prototype.</p></div></section>
<section id="decision"><p class="k">02 / Interaction design</p><h2>Make the reasons visible.</h2><p>The revised assessment separates four questions: does the identity match, are documents current, does the financial condition meet this property’s requirements, and do the resident’s preferences fit?</p><div class="outcome-grid"><div><h3>Earlier direction</h3><p>A single score on a pipeline card. The number did not explain why a case could move forward.</p></div><div><h3>Revised direction</h3><p>A readable status opens into criteria, supporting information and a specific follow-up action.</p></div></div>
<AllumoveQualification />
<div class="case-decision"><h3>Put the next action beside the problem</h3><p>The implemented component places “Request additional information” alongside the financial criterion that needs review. The adapted presentation above connects that unresolved criterion to its follow-up, so the reasoning stays visible.</p></div><div class="case-decision"><h3>Keep the rule attached to the property</h3><p>The panel states the applicable income requirement and its review context. Qualification describes a person–property relationship, rather than assigning a universal rating to a person.</p></div></section>
<section id="implementation"><p class="k">03 / Engineering</p><h2>One assessment, two levels of detail.</h2><p>The React implementation derives the summary status from the individual criteria. Pipeline cards use a compact status chip; the review panel exposes the underlying information. Labels and icons support the color coding.</p><div class="architecture" aria-label="Qualification presentation"><div>Identity · Documents · Finances · Preferences</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Derived qualification status</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Pipeline summary ↔ Detailed review panel</div></div><p>The trade-off is deliberate: explicit criteria take more space than a score. Progressive disclosure keeps the pipeline compact while making the reasons available in the review panel.</p></section>
<section id="outcome"><p class="k">04 / Outcome</p><h2>A decision people can inspect.</h2><p>The prototype demonstrates the translation from an ambiguous score to structured criteria, status and follow-up. It makes the product’s reasoning visible in the interface.</p><p class="case-note">The presentation above adapts the implemented component using fictional data. It does not establish live qualification, integrated customer records or measured conversion improvements. The next validation is whether brokers can identify the unresolved criterion and choose the appropriate follow-up without assistance.</p></section>
</Case>
